287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 244 0 R
/T (Modern enterprise environments generate logs at machine speed. A typical SOC receives thousands of alerts daily, with only a fraction ever reviewed. A large organisation's SOC might receive nearly 12,000 security alerts per day, of which only about 400 are actually reviewed, and perhaps 15 turn out to be real threats. The rest is noise: false positives, duplicate alerts, routine activity flagged as suspicious.)
/E (Modern enterprise environments generate logs at machine speed. A typical SOC receives thousands of alerts daily, with only a fraction ever reviewed. A large organisation's SOC might receive nearly 12,000 security alerts per day, of which only about 400 are actually reviewed, and perhaps 15 turn out to be real threats. The rest is noise: false positives, duplicate alerts, routine activity flagged as suspicious.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 244 0 R
/T (When traditional monitoring persists, organisations face compounding risk. Threat actors deliberately design attacks that don't match known patterns, use legitimate tools to blend in, and exploit the exact gaps where automation stops working. Without modernisation, the SOC becomes a cost centre that provides a false sense of security rather than a resilience engine.)
/E (When traditional monitoring persists, organisations face compounding risk. Threat actors deliberately design attacks that don't match known patterns, use legitimate tools to blend in, and exploit the exact gaps where automation stops working. Without modernisation, the SOC becomes a cost centre that provides a false sense of security rather than a resilience engine.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 244 0 R
/T (The constant filtering of noise is at the heart of SOC alert fatigue. Analysts spending hours triaging irrelevant alerts leads to burnout, high turnover, and real threats getting lost in the noise. Burnout among analysts is high, turnover is constant, and teams are stuck in a loop of reacting instead of improving.)
/E (The constant filtering of noise is at the heart of SOC alert fatigue. Analysts spending hours triaging irrelevant alerts leads to burnout, high turnover, and real threats getting lost in the noise. Burnout among analysts is high, turnover is constant, and teams are stuck in a loop of reacting instead of improving.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
290 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 244 0 R
/T (Traditional monitoring typically alerts after an event has occurred. Modern threats demand proactive detection and automated response. Attackers are becoming increasingly agile, with dwell time dropping from ~229 days a decade ago to less than 10 days today. More sophisticated threats need only a few hours.)
/E (Traditional monitoring typically alerts after an event has occurred. Modern threats demand proactive detection and automated response. Attackers are becoming increasingly agile, with dwell time dropping from ~229 days a decade ago to less than 10 days today. More sophisticated threats need only a few hours.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [18]
>>
